  <section>
    <p>Remark 2.3. The classical Catalan numbers <span data-field="C_n_value">{{ C_n_value }}</span> occur on the diagonal of both Catalan and Borel's triangles as well as in the first column of Borel's triangle.</p>
    <p>Remark 2.4. Borel's triangle appears in slightly different forms in the Online En-cyclopedia of Integer Sequences <span data-field="OEIS_link">{{ OEIS_link }}</span>. The triangle with alternating signs, and P. Barry gives an explicit form for Borel's triangle in the comments, noting that</p>
    <p>
      <span style="display: inline-block; margin-right: 20px;">
        <span style="font-size: 1.2em;">f_{n,k} = \frac{1}{n+1} \binom{2n}{n-k} \binom{2n-2k}{n-k}.</span>
      </span>
    </p>
    <p>The entry <span data-field="OEIS_number">{{ OEIS_number }}</span>, written in triangular form, has an additional column on the left of the triangle and the entries of the rows reversed from Borel's triangle. This sequence arises in Barry's recent work on generalized Pascal matrices defined by Riordan arrays; see, in particular, <span data-field="reference_3">{{ reference_3 }}</span>. </p>
    <p>Let <span data-field="T_b">{{ T_b }}</span> be a binary tree. As we work only with binary trees, we often drop the word "binary." Recall that <span data-field="T_root">{{ T_root }}</span> has a unique root vertex, which we call <span data-field="root_vertex">{{ root_vertex }}</span>. If <span data-field="v">{{ v }}</span> and <span data-field="w">{{ w }}</span> are two vertices of <span data-field="T_b">{{ T_b }}</span>, we say that <span data-field="v">{{ v }}</span> is a descendant of <span data-field="w">{{ w }}</span> if <span data-field="w">{{ w }}</span> is on the unique path from <span data-field="r">{{ r }}</span> to <span data-field="v">{{ v }}</span>. In this case, we also say that <span data-field="v">{{ v }}</span> is a descendant of <span data-field="w">{{ w }}</span>. If in addition <span data-field="v">{{ v }}</span> and <span data-field="w">{{ w }}</span> are the endpoints of an edge of <span data-field="T_b">{{ T_b }}</span>, then <span data-field="w">{{ w }}</span> is called an child of <span data-field="v">{{ v }}</span> and <span data-field="v">{{ v }}</span> is called the parent of <span data-field="w">{{ w }}</span>.</p>
    <p>If <span data-field="e">{{ e }}</span> is an edge of <span data-field="T_b">{{ T_b }}</span> with positive (resp., negative) slope, we say <span data-field="e">{{ e }}</span> is a left (resp., right) edge. If <span data-field="v">{{ v }}</span> is a child of <span data-field="w">{{ w }}</span>, we define the notion of a right child analogously. If <span data-field="v">{{ v }}</span> is a descendant of a left child of <span data-field="w">{{ w }}</span>, we say that <span data-field="v">{{ v }}</span> is a left descendant of <span data-field="w">{{ w }}</span> and define a right descendant similarly.</p>
    <p>A vertex of <span data-field="T_b">{{ T_b }}</span> with no children is a leaf, and we call a vertex branching if it has two children.</p>
  </section>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <section>
    <h3>Definition 2.5.</h3>
    <p>Let <span data-field="T_b">{{ T_b }}</span> be a binary tree. The rightmost leaf of <span data-field="T_b">{{ T_b }}</span> is the leaf obtained by starting at the root and descending, taking a right edge whenever possible. Let <span data-field="X">{{ X }}</span> be a set of leaves of <span data-field="T_b">{{ T_b }}</span> not containing <span data-field="T_rightmost_leaf">{{ T_rightmost_leaf }}</span>'s rightmost leaf. Then the pair <span data-field="T_X">{{ T_X }}</span> is called a leaf-marked tree. We call the vertices in <span data-field="X">{{ X }}</span> marked, and the other vertices unmarked.</p>
    <p>If <span data-field="B">{{ B }}</span> is a set of branching vertices of <span data-field="T_b">{{ T_b }}</span>, we call the pair <span data-field=" (T, B) ">{{  (T, B)  }}</span> a branch-marked tree.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>3. LEAF-MARKED TREES AND EK-SYMBOLS</h2>
    <p>We often use <span data-field="T">{{ T }}</span> to refer both to the tree and its set of vertices. For instance, we may write <span data-field="v &in; T">{{ v &in; T }}</span> if <span data-field="v">{{ v }}</span> is a vertex of <span data-field="T">{{ T }}</span> not contained in <span data-field="X">{{ X }}</span>.</p>
    <p><h3>2.1. Borel ideals.</h3></p>
    <p>All our ideals are in the ring <span data-field="S_ring">{{ S_ring }}</span> for a field <span data-field="k">{{ k }}</span>. If <span data-field="m">{{ m }}</span> is a monomial and <span data-field="x_i">{{ x_i }}</span> divides <span data-field="m">{{ m }}</span>, replacing <span data-field="x_i">{{ x_i }}</span> with <span data-field="x_i/x_i">{{ x_i/x_i }}</span> for some <span data-field="i">{{ i }}</span> <span data-field="< i">{{ < i }}</span> is called a Borel move.</p>
    <p><h3>Definition 2.6. A Borel ideal.</h3></p>
    <p>A Borel ideal <span data-field="I">{{ I }}</span> is a monomial ideal closed under Borel moves. We write <span data-field="I_n">{{ I_n }}</span> to denote the smallest Borel ideal containing the monomial <span data-field="m_n">{{ m_n }}</span>, for which we write <span data-field="gens(I_n)">{{ gens(I_n) }}</span>.</p>
    <p>Fact 2.7. The number of monomials in <span data-field="gens(I_n)">{{ gens(I_n) }}</span> is <span data-field="C_n_fact">{{ C_n_fact }}</span>, the <span data-field="n_th_catalan">{{ n_th_catalan }}</span> Catalan number.</p>
    <p>For example, there are <span data-field="C_3_value">{{ C_3_value }}</span> generators of <span data-field="I_3">{{ I_3 }}</span>: <span data-field="x_1^3 x_2^2 x_3^1, x_1^2 x_2^3 x_3^1, x_1^2 x_2^2 x_3^2, x_1 x_2^3 x_3^2, x_1^2 x_2 x_3^3</span>.</p>
    <p>If <span data-field="m">{{ x_1^3 x_2^2 x_3^1, x_1^2 x_2^3 x_3^1, x_1^2 x_2^2 x_3^2, x_1 x_2^3 x_3^2, x_1^2 x_2 x_3^3</span>.</p>
    <p>If <span data-field= }}</span> is a monomial, we let <span data-field="max(m)">{{ max(m) }}</span> denote the greatest index of a variable dividing <span data-field="m">{{ m }}</span>.</p>
    <p><h3>Definition 2.8. An Elshaun-Keravai symbol (or EK-symbol) of <span data-field="I_n">{{ I_n }}</span>.</h3></p>
    <p>A pair <span data-field=" (m, a) ">{{  (m, a)  }}</span> where <span data-field="m">{{ m }}</span> and <span data-field="a">{{ a }}</span> is a squarefree monomial with <span data-field="max(m)">{{ max(m) }}</span>. The (homological) degree of an EK-symbol (m, a) is the degree of <span data-field="a">{{ a }}</span>.</p>
    <p>The EK-symbols of a Borel ideal give a basis for its resolution. See <span data-field="reference_4">{{ reference_4 }}</span> for a proof and for <span data-field="PS_link">{{ PS_link }}</span> for a mapping cone approach to resolving a Borel ideal minimally. Thus we can calculate the Betti numbers of <span data-field="I_n">{{ I_n }}</span> by counting EK-symbols.</p>
    <p>Theorem 2.9 (See, for instance, <span data-field="reference_5">{{ reference_5 }}</span>). The <span data-field="n_th_betti_number">{{ n_th_betti_number }}</span> Betti number of <span data-field="I_n">{{ I_n }}</span> is the number of EK-symbols of <span data-field="I_n">{{ I_n }}</span> of homological degree <span data-field="d">{{ d }}</span>.</p>
  </section>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <section>
    <h3>3. LEAF-MARKED TREES AND EK-SYMBOLS (continued)</h3>
    <p>In this section, we construct a bijection between (2) and (3) in Theorem <span data-field="theorem_ref_3">{{ theorem_ref_3 }}</span>. Let <span data-field="T">{{ T }}</span> be a tree. Throughout, we refer to an ordering on the vertices of <span data-field="T">{{ T }}</span>, defined as follows. Given vertices <span data-field="v">{{ v }}</span> and <span data-field="w">{{ w }}</span>, let <span data-field="v &lt; w">{{ v &lt; w }}</span> if <span data-field="v">{{ v }}</span> is an ancestor of <span data-field="w">{{ w }}</span>, or if <span data-field="v">{{ v }}</span> and <span data-field="w">{{ w }}</span> are incomparable, we say that <span data-field="v">{{ v }}</span> is to the left of <span data-field="w">{{ w }}</span>. This defines a total order on the vertices of <span data-field="T">{{ T }}</span>. Moreover, this order is the transitive closure of the order defined by <span data-field="v &lt; w_edge">{{ v &lt; w_edge }}</span> whenever <span data-field="w">{{ w }}</span> is a right child of <span data-field="v">{{ v }}</span> or <span data-field="v">{{ v }}</span> is a left child of <span data-field="w">{{ w }}</span>.</p>
    <p><h3>Construction 3.1.</h3></p>
    <p>Let <span data-field="T_b">{{ T_b }}</span> be a leaf-marked tree on <span data-field="n_vertices_c31">{{ n_vertices_c31 }}</span> + <span data-field="k_marked_c31">{{ k_marked_c31 }}</span> vertices with <span data-field="X">{{ X }}</span> = <span data-field="k_marked_c31">{{ k_marked_c31 }}</span>. We define a labeling <span data-field="phi">{{ phi }}</span> as follows. For a vertex <span data-field="v &in; T">{{ v &in; T }}</span>, define <span data-field="φ(v)">{{ φ(v) }}</span> by:</p>
    <p>
      <span style="display: inline-block; margin-right: 20px;">
        <span style="font-size: 1.2em;">φ(v) = 1 + |{{ { w &in; T | w &lt; v &and; w is not a descendant of v } }}|</span>
      </span>
    </p>
  </section>
</div>

