        <p>5. FURTHER RESEARCH</p>
        <p>Theorem <span data-field="variable_j">{{ variable_j }}</span> gives several classes of objects counted by Borel's triangle. However,</p>
        <p>we believe the entries of Borel's triangle will prove useful in many other enumerative</p>
        <p>contexts. For example, Stanley's book <span data-field="variable_k">{{ variable_k }}</span> lists many sets counted by the <span data-field="variable_l">{{ variable_l }}</span>th Catalan</p>
        <p>number, including binary trees on <span data-field="variable_m">{{ variable_m }}</span> vertices, generators of Borel ideals <span data-field="variable_n">{{ variable_n }}</span> (implicit in</p>
        <p>the ballot-path bijection), and triangulations of <span data-field="variable_o">{{ variable_o }}</span> points in convex position. As these</p>
        <p>classes of objects (with some extra structure) correspond to marked trees, minimal</p>
        <p>syzygies of Borel ideals, and pointed pseudotriangulations, we anticipate that most</p>
        <p>objects on Stanley's list similarly correspond to a new class of objects counted by</p>
        <p>Borel's triangle.</p>

        <h3 class="section-heading">Open-ended Problem 5.1.</h3>
        <p>Find classes of objects counted by Borel's triangle. In</p>
        <p>particular, for each class of objects counted by the Catalan numbers, identify extra</p>
        <p>structure that defines a new class of objects counted by Borel's triangle.</p>

        <p>We address this problem by studying binary parenthesizations, which</p>
        <p>are counted by the Catalan numbers <span data-field="variable_p">{{ variable_p }}</span>. For example, there are <span data-field="variable_q">{{ variable_q }}</span> binary</p>
        <p>parenthesizations of <span data-field="variable_r">{{ variable_r }}</span> symbols:</p>
        <p>($<span data-field="variable_s">{{ variable_s }}</span><span data-field="variable_t">{{ variable_t }}</span>$)$($<span data-field="variable_u">{{ variable_u }}</span><span data-field="variable_v">{{ variable_v }}</span>$)$, ($<span data-field="variable_w">{{ variable_w }}</span>(<span data-field="variable_x">{{ variable_x }}</span><span data-field="variable_y">{{ variable_y }}</span>$)$<span data-field="variable_z">{{ variable_z }}</span>$), ($(<span data-field="variable_a">{{ variable_a }}</span><span data-field="variable_b">{{ variable_b }}</span>$)$<span data-field="variable_c">{{ variable_c }}</span>$)$<span data-field="variable_d">{{ variable_d }}</span>$, and ($<span data-field="variable_e">{{ variable_e }}</span>$($<span data-field="variable_f">{{ variable_f }}</span>(<span data-field="variable_g">{{ variable_g }}</span><span data-field="variable_h">{{ variable_h }}</span>$)$)$.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="page">
        <p style="text-align: right;"><span data-field="page_number">{{ page_number }}</span></p>
        <p>CHRISTOPHER A. FRANCISCO, JEFFREY MERMIN, AND JAY SCHWEIG</p>
        <p>Now let <span data-field="variable_i">{{ variable_i }}</span> &le; {<span data-field="variable_j">{{ variable_j }}</span>}. We let the <span data-field="variable_k">{{ variable_k }}</span>-word on <span data-field="variable_l">{{ variable_l }}</span> be the word obtained</p>
        <p>by doubling <span data-field="variable_m">{{ variable_m }}</span> whenever <span data-field="variable_n">{{ variable_n }}</span> &in; <span data-field="variable_X">{{ variable_X }}</span>. For example, if <span data-field="variable_X">{{ variable_X }}</span> = {<span data-field="variable_j">{{ variable_j }}</span>}, then</p>
        <p>the <span data-field="variable_k">{{ variable_k }}</span>-word on <span data-field="variable_l">{{ variable_l }}</span> is <span data-field="variable_m">{{ variable_m }}</span>. Similarly, an <span data-field="variable_X">{{ variable_X }}</span>-parenthesization on <span data-field="variable_n">{{ variable_n }}</span> is a</p>
        <p>binary parenthesization of the <span data-field="variable_l">{{ variable_l }}</span>-word on <span data-field="variable_n">{{ variable_n }}</span> satisfying the following property: For</p>
        <p>each doubled letter <span data-field="variable_m">{{ variable_m }}</span>, both types of parentheses appear between the two copies of</p>
        <p>the letter. For example, if <span data-field="variable_X">{{ variable_X }}</span> = {<span data-field="variable_j">{{ variable_j }}</span>}, then the <span data-field="variable_k">{{ variable_k }}</span>-word on <span data-field="variable_n">{{ variable_n }}</span> is <span data-field="variable_m">{{ variable_m }}</span>, and there are</p>
        <p>three <span data-field="variable_o">{{ variable_o }}</span>-parenthesizations on <span data-field="variable_n">{{ variable_n }}</span>.</p>

        <h3 class="section-heading">Theorem 5.2.</h3>
        <p>Recall that we write <span data-field="variable_p">{{ variable_p }}</span> for the <span data-field="variable_q">{{ variable_q }}</span>th entry in the <span data-field="variable_r">{{ variable_r }}</span>th row of Borel's</p>
        <p>triangle. Then <span data-field="variable_f_n">{{ variable_f_n }}</span> is the number of <span data-field="variable_s">{{ variable_s }}</span>-parenthesizations of <span data-field="variable_n">{{ variable_n }}</span>.</p>

        <p>For example, a symmetry argument coupled with the example above shows that</p>
        <p>the number of <span data-field="variable_k">{{ variable_k }}</span>-parenthesizations on <span data-field="variable_n">{{ variable_n }}</span> for which <span data-field="variable_X">{{ variable_X }}</span> = {<span data-field="variable_j">{{ variable_j }}</span>} is <span data-field="variable_f_4">{{ variable_f_4 }}</span>, while for <span data-field="variable_X">{{ variable_X }}</span> = {<span data-field="variable_2">{{ variable_2 }}</span>} is <span data-field="variable_f_4">{{ variable_f_4 }}</span>.</p>

        <p>Sketch of proof of Theorem <span data-field="variable_s">{{ variable_s }}</span>. A pointed pseudotriangulation of the single chain of length <span data-field="variable_n">{{ variable_n }}</span>,</p>
        <p>let <span data-field="variable_P">{{ variable_P }}</span>, is not connected to the tip. We associate to <span data-field="variable_P">{{ variable_P }}</span> the <span data-field="variable_X">{{ variable_X }}</span>-parenthesization</p>
        <p>obtained by attaching a left parenthesis to <span data-field="variable_x">{{ variable_x }}</span> for each edge of <span data-field="variable_P">{{ variable_P }}</span> having <span data-field="variable_a">{{ variable_a }}</span> as its left</p>
        <p>vertex, and attaching a right parenthesis to <span data-field="variable_x">{{ variable_x }}</span> for each edge of <span data-field="variable_P">{{ variable_P }}</span> having <span data-field="variable_b">{{ variable_b }}</span> as its right</p>
        <p>vertex. For example, let <span data-field="variable_P">{{ variable_P }}</span> be the pointed pseudotriangulation in Example <span data-field="variable_c">{{ variable_c }}</span>.</p>
        <p><span data-field="variable_X">{{ variable_X }}</span> = {<span data-field="variable_j">{{ variable_j }}</span>} and the associated <span data-field="variable_X">{{ variable_X }}</span>-parenthesization is (<span data-field="variable_d">{{ variable_d }}</span>(<span data-field="variable_e">{{ variable_e }}</span><span data-field="variable_f">{{ variable_f }}</span>$)$(<span data-field="variable_g">{{ variable_g }}</span>$)$).</p>

        <p>As marked trees count the minimal syzygies of <span data-field="variable_I_n">{{ variable_I_n }}</span>, one can ask whether the Catalan</p>
        <p>torsions yield an interesting differential. We can of course translate the EK differential</p>
        <p>along our bijections, but the resulting maps do not seem at all natural on sets of</p>
        <p>marked trees.</p>
    </div>

