        <p>symbol is <span data-field="variable_b">{{ variable_b }}</span> - <span data-field="variable_c">{{ variable_c }}</span> + <span data-field="variable_d">{{ variable_d }}</span>, which corresponds to the linear</p>
        <p>combination below:</p>
        <div style="text-align: center; margin: 20px 0;">
            <span data-field="variable_D(T)">{{ variable_D(T) }}</span>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="page">
        <p style="text-align: right;"><span data-field="page_number">{{ page_number }}</span></p>
        <p>As this figure illustrates, there is no obvious combinatorial rule for defining the</p>
        <p>differential on a marked tree.</p>
        <p>While the Eliahou-Kervaire differentials do not appear to make sense on the space</p>
        <p>of marked trees, it is possible that there is a natural combinatorial differential. Such</p>
        <p>a structure would be of considerable interest to algebraists; for example, in <span data-field="variable_N">{{ variable_N }}</span>,</p>
        <p>other bases for the resolution of a Borel ideal are constructed. Unfortunately, this</p>
        <p>far we have been unable to find a reasonable differential on either trees or the other</p>
        <p>structures discussed in this paper. For example, on the space of <span data-field="variable_X">{{ variable_X }}</span>-parenthesizations,</p>
        <p>the following map looks tantalizingly like a differential, but it is not.</p>

        <h3 class="section-heading">Example 5.4.</h3>
        <p>For an <span data-field="variable_X">{{ variable_X }}</span>-parenthesization with <span data-field="variable_n">{{ variable_n }}</span> &ge; <span data-field="variable_j">{{ variable_j }}</span>, define <span data-field="variable_L(w)">{{ variable_L(w) }}</span> to be the (<span data-field="variable_X">{{ variable_X }}</span></p>
        <p>j-parenthesization obtained by deleting the first copy of <span data-field="variable_x">{{ variable_x }}</span> from <span data-field="variable_w">{{ variable_w }}</span> and simplifying</p>
        <p>(or, if this is not an <span data-field="variable_X">{{ variable_X }}</span>-parenthesization, set <span data-field="variable_L(w)">{{ variable_L(w) }}</span> equal to zero). Similarly</p>
        <p>define <span data-field="variable_R(w)">{{ variable_R(w) }}</span> by deleting the second copy of <span data-field="variable_x">{{ variable_x }}</span>. If <span data-field="variable_w">{{ variable_w }}</span> = (<span data-field="variable_x">{{ variable_x }}</span><span data-field="variable_y">{{ variable_y }}</span>$)$(<span data-field="variable_z">{{ variable_z }}</span><span data-field="variable_a">{{ variable_a }}</span>$)$,</p>
        <p>then <span data-field="variable_L(w)">{{ variable_L(w) }}</span> = (<span data-field="variable_x">{{ variable_x }}</span><span data-field="variable_y">{{ variable_y }}</span>$)$(<span data-field="variable_z">{{ variable_z }}</span><span data-field="variable_a">{{ variable_a }}</span>$)$ and <span data-field="variable_R(w)">{{ variable_R(w) }}</span> = (<span data-field="variable_x">{{ variable_x }}</span><span data-field="variable_y">{{ variable_y }}</span>$)$(<span data-field="variable_z">{{ variable_z }}</span><span data-field="variable_a">{{ variable_a }}</span>$)$.</p>
        <p>The definition of <span data-field="variable_L">{{ variable_L }}</span> looks like a boundary operator, and appears to mimic the Eliahou-</p>
        <p>Kervaire differential, but it is not a differential. In fact, with as above, <span data-field="variable_D^2(w)">{{ variable_D^2(w) }}</span> &ne; <span data-field="variable_w">{{ variable_w }}</span>.</p>

        <h3 class="section-heading">Question 5.5.</h3>
        <p>Among the classes of objects counted by Borel's triangle, is there one</p>
        <p>that admits a natural differential structure?</p>
    </div>

